## A. Finite order of the degree-zero period action, not of R(s)
Fix w|ell, its actual chart j and s with IsWeilPair(j(s),0). Write beta(s)
for the Bcris-linear operator on the potential-crystalline periods extended
to Bcris tensor the coefficient representation. Native potential
crystallinity means that periods fixed near identity span. Choose a finite
spanning family for the finite-dimensional coefficient representation
over Bcris, or the native finite expansion of a module basis in
eventually fixed periods (RegularityFrobenius 195–202). Intersect the
finitely many identity neighborhoods. On that neighborhood beta(t,0)=1
for inertial t, by its defining action on the fixed periods and linearity.
Since nearOne is pulled back along j into Gp and Gp is profinite, the
chosen neighborhood contains an open subgroup in Gp. Its intersection
with the cyclic group generated by j(s) has finite index: indeed the
open subgroup has finite index in compact Gp. Thus some n>=1 has
j(s)^n in the neighborhood. All powers s^n remain degree-zero Weil pairs.
By the actual multiplicativity theorem beta(s)^n=beta(s^n)=1.
This argument requires neither finite order of s nor finite order of R(s).
It also does not require a uniform n in s or w, or compactness of the
abstract domain group. Coefficient endomorphisms commute with beta by
RegularityFrobenius 158–159; otherwise noncommutative polynomial evaluation
would not be a ring homomorphism.

## B. Exact radical transfer by a single Bezout identity
Let K=Qbar_ell, A the actual endomorphism ring, f:K[X] -> A the
coefficient/operator evaluation homomorphism. Let P kill beta, let
q be the image of the O-polynomial Q, and assume each root of P in K
is a root of q. Put H=X^n-1, G=monic gcd(P,H). H is squarefree
(characteristic zero, n positive, derivative nX^(n-1) relatively prime
to H). Every root of G is a root of P; G is squarefree and splits in K.
Consequently G divides q. Bezout gives G=A0*H+B0*P and q=C*G, hence

    q = C*(A0*(X^n-1)+B0*P).

Apply f: f(H)=f(P)=0, so f(q)=0. This remains valid when P=0 or P
is a nonzero constant; use gcd, do not divide by P. No norms, spectral
estimates, diagonalization of a semilinear operator, or integral
factorization. Work over K, then descend, not over O by assuming O[X]
is a PID. Native lemma map_eq_zero_of_roots is exactly this statement.
The explicit algebra certificate below isolates the cancellation.
The native root lemma is a separate prepared comparison, not a claim
that its hypotheses follow solely from the existence of P.

